<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurybiades</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/480-bce">480 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eurybiades was the Spartan admiral who commanded the allied Greek fleet during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> in 480 BCE. Although the Athenian contingent was by far the largest, the allies insisted on a Spartan commander. Persuaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, he fought at Artemisium and then at Salamis, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> defeated the Persian fleet.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eurybiades was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurycleides">Eurycleides</a>. He was a Spartan navarch, and in 480 BCE he was chosen to command the Greek fleet against the second Persian invasion because the Peloponnesian city-states led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> were wary of the growing power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as a whole would not serve under an Athenian, despite the Athenians' superior naval skill, but for all the enmity between the two cities Eurybiades came to rely on the help of the Athenian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first act as commander was to take the fleet to Artemisium, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, to meet the Persian navy. Finding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> already there, he ordered a retreat, although the Euboeans begged him to stay. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, the Euboeans then bribed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> to keep the fleet in place, and Themistocles passed part of the bribe to Eurybiades. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-artemisium">Battle of Artemisium</a> that followed was indecisive, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> withdrew their ships to the island of Salamis.</p><p>At Salamis Eurybiades at first wanted to move the fleet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a>, where the armies of the Hellenic League were building fortifications. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> wanted to force a naval battle in the straits and had to threaten to withdraw the Athenian ships, the largest contingent, to keep him there. Once Themistocles had tricked the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> into sending part of his fleet round the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, Eurybiades was forced to accept Salamis as the battlefield, and the battle ended in a decisive Greek victory.</p><p>After Salamis Eurybiades opposed pursuing the Persian fleet and sailing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> to destroy the bridge of ships that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> had built there. He preferred that Xerxes be able to escape, rather than stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and perhaps renew the war on land. On his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> he was rewarded with an olive wreath for the victory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> received a similar honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eurybiades” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The alliance had a council of delegates (probouloi) meeting at the Isthmus, but command lay with Sparta, which led on land and, through <b>Eurybiades</b>, at sea, although Themistocles' Athenian fleet was the largest.</blockquote><a href="/state/hellenic-alliance">Hellenic Alliance</a></li></ul></section>
